WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.178
Nonessential Elements: Commas, Dashes, Parentheses

00:00:05.558 --> 00:00:08.154
Extra info gets a matching pair of marks.

00:00:09.354 --> 00:00:10.352
How to spot it

00:00:10.752 --> 00:00:13.325
A comma or dash already opens a phrase

00:00:13.875 --> 00:00:15.653
Choices that close it differently

00:00:16.203 --> 00:00:19.936
It’s testing: where extra information starts and ends

00:00:21.136 --> 00:00:21.931
The method

00:00:22.331 --> 00:00:24.785
Step 1: Find the extra info

00:00:25.066 --> 00:00:26.151
Could you delete it?

00:00:26.701 --> 00:00:28.918
Step 2: Delete it in your head

00:00:29.198 --> 00:00:30.948
Is the sentence still complete?

00:00:31.498 --> 00:00:34.020
Step 3: Match the opening mark

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:38.251
Dash opens? Dash closes. Comma? Comma.

00:00:38.801 --> 00:00:41.934
Step 4: Title + name = no commas

00:00:42.214 --> 00:00:43.881
biologist Rachel Carson

00:00:44.431 --> 00:00:45.541
In a few words

00:00:45.691 --> 00:00:48.186
Find it. Delete it. Match it.

00:00:49.386 --> 00:00:50.209
The rules

00:00:50.609 --> 00:00:51.631
A matching pair

00:00:51.911 --> 00:00:56.063
Wrong: The Nile, Africa’s longest river flows north.

00:00:56.343 --> 00:01:00.541
Right: The Nile, Africa’s longest river, flows north.

00:01:01.091 --> 00:01:02.331
Never mix marks

00:01:02.611 --> 00:01:05.660
Wrong: The lake — the deepest, freezes.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.987
Right: The lake — the deepest — freezes.

00:01:09.537 --> 00:01:12.114
Title before a name: no commas

00:01:12.394 --> 00:01:14.923
Wrong: astronaut, Sally Ride

00:01:15.203 --> 00:01:17.370
Right: astronaut Sally Ride

00:01:17.919 --> 00:01:20.592
That = essential, which = extra

00:01:20.872 --> 00:01:24.143
Right: The Nile, which flows north, is long.

00:01:25.343 --> 00:01:26.384
Let’s try one

00:01:26.784 --> 00:01:34.053
The Svalbard Global Seed Vault — a facility built deep inside a mountain on a remote Norwegian island

00:01:34.053 --> 00:01:39.359
______ stores backup copies of more than a million seed samples from around the world.

00:01:40.009 --> 00:01:43.862
Here’s the extra information. It opens with a dash.

00:01:44.412 --> 00:01:50.022
Delete it: the seed vault stores backup copies. That’s still a complete sentence.

00:01:50.572 --> 00:01:54.917
It opened with a dash, so it must close with a dash. Choice B.

00:01:56.117 --> 00:01:57.113
Common traps

00:01:57.513 --> 00:01:58.707
Mismatched marks

00:01:58.987 --> 00:02:01.618
Opened with a dash, closed with a comma.

00:02:02.168 --> 00:02:03.308
The missing closer

00:02:03.588 --> 00:02:05.755
Only one comma around the extra info.

00:02:06.955 --> 00:02:11.533
Nonessential Elements: Commas, Dashes, Parentheses

00:02:11.813 --> 00:02:14.308
Find it. Delete it. Match it.

00:02:14.858 --> 00:02:15.631
Spot it

00:02:15.831 --> 00:02:19.582
A comma or dash opens a phrase; choices close it

00:02:20.132 --> 00:02:20.839
Steps

00:02:21.039 --> 00:02:23.041
1. Find the extra info

00:02:23.321 --> 00:02:25.894
2. Delete it: still complete?

00:02:26.174 --> 00:02:28.100
3. Close with the same mark

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.415
4. Title + name: no commas

00:02:31.965 --> 00:02:32.717
Remember

00:02:32.917 --> 00:02:38.717
Extra info: matching pair (, , — — ( ))

00:02:38.997 --> 00:02:40.793
Never mix a dash and a comma

00:02:41.073 --> 00:02:43.332
astronaut Sally Ride (no commas)

00:02:43.612 --> 00:02:46.979
that = essential; which = extra (commas)

00:02:47.529 --> 00:02:48.134
Trap

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:50.966
Opened with a dash, closed with a comma

00:02:51.716 --> 00:02:55.410
